Danielle Jacobs
December 7, 2025

Purpose:
The gerp project takes in an input directory, and allows the user to search
for any word or series of words within that directory. All occurrences of 
that word, along with their file name and line numbers and the full line of
text, are printed to an output file. Searches can be case sensitive or case
insensitive. The program can be quit, and a new output file can be named
and created.

Acknowledgments:
I went to office hours with Daniel and Chris.
I received help, advice, and suggestions from Ariel Jacobs.
I talked to the class chatbot.

Files:
- Processing.h:
    Cleans words and traverses directories.

- Processing.cpp: 
    Strip non alpha numeric characters from words, and directory traversal
    to return the names of all the files in the directory.

- Gerp.cpp
    Defines how new words are added and stored, how the files are processed
    and searched, and how input from the user is routed.

- Gerp.h
    Gerp can interact with files, store words from an input directory to
    make them easily searchable later.

- WordTable.h
    The word table acts as the hash table for this project. It stores all words
    found in the input directory at the hashMod of their lowercase spelling.

- WordTable.cpp
    The load factor (how filled the word table is) can be checked, as well as
    the current size and capacity. New words, with no existing matches,
    can be added.

- WordTableEntry.h
    A word table entry is a lowercase spelling of a word and all its contained
    information, including the various different spelling/casings of a word
    and where they were found in the input directory.

- WordTableEntry.cpp
    Different spellings (case variations) of a word can be added to the 
    relevant word table entry.

- main.cpp
    When the startup command is submitted through the terminal, starts the 
    gerp program and supplies it the starting information from the user 
    (input directory and initial output file).

- Makefile: 
    Defines how ./unit_tests and ./gerp are compiled and run.

- unit_tests.cpp
    Tests that word table entries can properly be accessed and searched, that
    words are cleaned properly, that output matches the reference both in 
    the command line and the output file, that load factor and expansion 
    work as expected.

Compile and run:
    compile: make
    run: ./gerp inputDirectory outputFile

Architectural overview:
    Gerp is the main class with most of the functionality. Main simply runs the 
    program. Within gerp, there is a WordTable (hash table) that contains
    vectors (collision lists) of WordTableEntries at each hashMod.
    A wordTableEntry has a spelling in all lowercase, and information about
    each variation of that spelling and all the places they can be found
    in the input directory.

Data structures:
    My program is built on the standard hash function, as well as many vectors
    of vectors. Standard hash is known to be robust and well distributed.
    My outer vector: one entry for each hashmod (the std hash of the 
    word mod the hash table capacity). Next vector: one entry for each word
    found at that hashmod. Within those WordTableEntries, I keep a few more 
    vectors: a vector of various spellings, which each contain vectors 
    with the line numbers and file IDs where they can be found.
    I chose vectors whenever possible because indexing into them provides an
    O(1) search time, which was essential for this search program. I did not
    store all the files from the input directory in memory, even though I
    started with that approach thinking it might save time on lookup.
    However opening them and reading them each time saved a ton of memory 
    and only slightly increased the time. 

    The other option discussed in class to handle collisions in a hash table
    was linear probing. I found this easier to think about and keep track of
    where I might find an entry.

    I also tried storing my hashtable on the heap but found many values were
    getting initialized with garbage and discussed with Chris during office ours
    that I could have even largeGutenberg on the stack, because the std vector
    handles a lot of the heap/stack allocation. I still need to pass pointers
    around when I want to update existing word table entries, rather than
    meaningless copies.

